<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 6: Data Shapes                                           -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Opens with Sierra Leone's Ebola data management (Owada et al.    -->
<!--  2016): two unlinked systems, district files in different shapes, -->
<!--  and a case fatality rate that was biased in both directions.     -->
<!--  The R section then covers:                                       -->
<!--    - reshaping (flamingos; the JHU COVID-19 wide files)           -->
<!--    - appending (Kenya road accidents 2016 + 2017; fertiliser       -->
<!--      voucher lists; Kenya vs Uganda fatality definitions)          -->
<!--    - merging (road accidents + 2019 census; Homa Bay EMR systems;  -->
<!--      the IEBC voter register)                                      -->
<!--  Outstanding: the STACK question, and the TODO in ss-merging-keys. -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-data-shapes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Data Shapes</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-data-shapes">
    <p>
      In <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/>, adding dimensions
      to a plot worked so nicely because every dataset was already laid out the
      way a good plotting system expects: one row per observation, and one
      column per variable.
      Add a variable, add an aesthetic mapping.
      But that only works if the data is already in the right
      <term>shape</term>.
      Real data hardly ever arrives like that.
      And the same numbers, arranged differently, can be really easy to work
      with, or almost impossible, depending on how they're laid out.
      So in this chapter, we want to convince you that the shape of a dataset
      really matters, and deserves just as much thought as the numbers inside
      it.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: The opener, on its own, told in full                  -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-intro-data-shapes">
    <title>The Same Information, the Wrong Shape</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, we followed one organisation
        managing data across five Ebola treatment units.
        That story was about data quality: forms damaged by chlorine, values
        misheard across a fence, and the same symptom written down under three
        different names.
        Every one of those problems was inside a single record.
      </p>

      <p>
        Owada et al. (2016) describe a different problem, in Sierra Leone.
        Here, the information needed to run the response did exist.
        But it had been recorded in shapes that didn't fit together, so it
        couldn't be combined.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-two-systems">
      <title>One patient, two records</title>

      <p>
        Each Ebola case in Sierra Leone was supposed to be recorded on a paper
        case investigation form (CIF), filled in by a surveillance officer in
        the field.
        That form was then entered into the Epi Info VHF database, a tool built
        by the US Centers for Disease Control specifically for tracking outbreak
        cases and their contacts.
        In theory, several people could enter data at the same time, and
        district databases could be merged into one national database.
      </p>

      <p>
        In practice, it wasn't that simple.
        Three main things went wrong.
        First, most district data managers had never used the software before.
        Second, once duplicate records had been created, they were hard to find,
        and even harder to merge.
        And third, the paper forms often reached the district response centres
        days after the case had been investigated, so the national database was
        never fully up to date, even late in the outbreak.
      </p>

      <p>
        But the failure with the biggest consequences was much quieter than any
        of these.
        When a blood sample came back from the lab, the result was often entered
        as a brand new record, instead of being added to that patient's existing
        record.
        So one person, with one infection, now had two rows in the same
        database, and nothing to link them or show they were the same person.
      </p>

      <p>
        On top of the Epi Info database, district teams also kept a second,
        completely separate line list in Excel.
        This was built mainly from lab sample results, and held some of the same
        information: age, sex, location, and the dates each sample was
        collected, received, and tested.
        Teams used it because it was quicker for producing daily reports and
        charts.
        So a single patient might now be in the national database (possibly
        twice!) and in a district spreadsheet, with no reliable way to tell
        which entries in the two systems were the same person.
      </p>

      <p>
        None of this is a data quality problem in the way we talked about in
        <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>.
        The problem was structural: two systems collecting overlapping
        information about the same people, with no agreed identifier, and no
        process for reconciling them.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-districts">
      <title>Every district its own shape</title>

      <p>
        As the outbreak grew, District Ebola Response Centres (DERCs) were set
        up across the country.
        Each one was responsible for its own area: who had tested positive, who
        they'd been in contact with, who had died, and what the lab results
        said.
        Collecting data close to where the cases were meant that local teams
        could act on what they saw, without waiting for instructions from the
        capital.
      </p>

      <p>
        And each centre had to manage a constant daily stream of information on
        top of the case forms.
        Live alerts, recording who had been seen and whether a sample had been
        taken.
        Death alerts, noting whether a swab had been collected and a burial team
        sent out.
        Quarantined households, and whether they had food.
        Confirmed positive counts from health facilities.
        Most centres tracked all of this on manual dashboards (physical displays
        showing what was happening in their district right now), and then copied
        the numbers into Excel sheets or Word documents.
      </p>

      <p>
        So every district had data, but each one had structured it in its own
        way, with its own column names, definitions, and meanings.
        In one district's dataset, a row might be a patient.
        In another, a row might be a day, with columns counting how many new
        cases and deaths there were that day.
        And in a third, confirmed and suspected cases might be on completely
        separate sheets.
      </p>

      <p>
        We want to be really clear that no district was doing anything wrong
        here.
        A team designing a spreadsheet for their own district's daily use has no
        reason to think about how a national team might later want to combine it
        with everyone else's.
        The incompatibility only showed up when someone tried to put the files
        together.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can't stack a file where each row is a patient on top of a file
        where each row is a day.
        If you put one underneath the other, you get a table where the rows mean
        two different things, and any count you take from it is meaningless.
        A column called <c>EVD_positive</c> in one district's file and a column
        called <c>confirmed_cases</c> in another's might hold the same thing, or
        might not, and there was no documentation to settle it.
        So before the national team could count anything at all, someone had to
        answer one question: what shape should this data be in?
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-cfr">
      <title>The number that was wrong in both directions</title>

      <p>
        To see what this cost, let's follow a single number.
        Out of everyone who caught Ebola, what fraction died?
        Epidemiologists call this the <term>case fatality rate</term>, and in an
        outbreak, it's about as fundamental as numbers get.
        It tells you how deadly the disease is in this population, whether
        treatment is helping, and whether things are getting better or worse.
        It's confirmed deaths divided by confirmed cases.
      </p>

      <p>
        Both halves of that fraction were unreliable, and they went wrong in
        ways that pushed the answer in opposite directions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let's start with deaths.
        To confirm that a death was caused by Ebola, you needed a post-mortem
        mouth swab.
        In areas where traditional burial practices carried on, that swab often
        wasn't taken.
        Those deaths were real, and they were caused by Ebola, but they never
        made it into the count of confirmed deaths.
        If your count of deaths is too low, and you divide it by the count of
        cases, the fraction comes out too small.
        The outbreak looks less deadly than it really is.
      </p>

      <p>
        So let's try to correct for that.
        One sensible fix is to only use cases where we know the outcome
        (recovered or died), and ignore everyone who's still being treated.
        That takes out the patients whose outcome hasn't been decided yet.
        But deaths and recoveries weren't recorded on the same timescale.
        A death could be entered on the day it happened.
        A recovery could only be entered once the patient's hospital stay had
        completely finished, which took a lot longer.
        So at any moment, the cases with a known outcome included a bigger share
        of deaths than they would once everyone's outcome was known.
        Only using known outcomes pushes the fraction too high.
        Now the outbreak looks more deadly than it really is.
      </p>

      <p>
        And underneath both of these calculations, there was a third
        complication.
        Even before Ebola arrived, malaria accounted for roughly 38% of hospital
        admissions in Sierra Leone, and about 30% of deaths in children under
        five.
        In their early stages, malaria and Ebola have similar symptoms.
        That overlap probably delayed the recognition of some real Ebola cases,
        and it also meant that some malaria deaths risked being counted as Ebola
        deaths.
        So the same confusion was moving the numbers in both directions, in
        different places, at the same time.
      </p>

      <p>
        Both answers were systematically biased, in opposite directions, by the
        same underlying problem: records about the same patient couldn't be
        reliably linked and combined.
        The death that needed to go on top of the fraction was sitting somewhere
        in the lab records.
        The case it belonged to was sitting somewhere in the case database.
        Without a shared identifier to connect them, there was no way to
        calculate the fraction correctly.
      </p>

      <p>
        The national team came to the obvious conclusion.
        The system that was supposed to hold the complete picture couldn't be
        trusted to give them one.
        So for the rest of the outbreak, they stopped relying on the case
        database for routine reporting, and used lab data for their day-to-day
        situation reports instead.
        The case data was never fully reconciled.
        When the outbreak ended, lots of suspected cases were left permanently
        unclassified, never confirmed as Ebola, and never ruled out.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ebola-shape-lesson">
      <title>Shape is a decision made before the data arrives</title>

      <p>
        Owada et al. (2016) are really direct about the cause.
        <q>Lack of consistency</q> in how data was structured across districts
        <q>caused problems with merging and aggregating cases to report critical
        information.</q>
        And that critical information sounds almost mundane.
        How many new alerts today?
        How many new confirmed cases?
        How many blood tests were processed, and what did they show?
        How many deaths were there in each district?
        But these are the numbers that decide where treatment units get built,
        where contact tracers get sent, and where the response is losing ground.
        When the files underneath can't be combined automatically, just
        producing those numbers becomes a manual job.
      </p>

      <p>
        And the loss went beyond the response at the time.
        The authors conclude that <q>much of the data has never found its way
        into any structured database and may never be recoverable.</q>
        That's data that was collected at real cost, and real risk, in formats
        that each worked fine on their own, but couldn't be used together.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two of the paper's recommendations tackle this directly.
        The first is regular data harmonisation meetings between district
        monitoring and evaluation officers and national and external data
        managers, to improve data quality and consistency at both levels.
        The second is training for surveillance officers and data clerks in how
        to collect and enter data.
        Both of these are really recommendations about shape: agreeing what a
        row means, what a column is called, and how files from different places
        will eventually be brought together.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's what this chapter is about: the operations that bring data
        together, and the decisions you need to make before those operations can
        work.
        If everyone had shared an understanding of what shape the national
        reporting needed, much of what was lost could have been recovered.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Owada, K., Eckmanns, T., Kamara, K.O.B.
        and Olu, O.O.
        (2016).
        <q>Epidemiological Data Management during an
      Outbreak of Ebola Virus Disease: Key Issues and Observations
      from Sierra Leone.</q>
        <em>Frontiers in Public Health</em>, 4, 163.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
