  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: Applying and looking at different shapes              -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-shapes-applied">
    <title>Reshaping Data in R</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In the rest of this chapter, we work through these shape problems in R,
        using a few different datasets (flamingos, the Kenya road accidents
        data, and the Kenya census).
        Along the way, we also look at some real examples of each problem out in
        the wild.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-long-to-wide">
      <title>Wide or Long?</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          The same data can be arranged in really different ways, depending on
          who needs to use it:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Long format</term> is one row per observation, one column
                per variable.
                This is the shape that plotting and analysis tools expect.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Wide format</term> is one column per category, one row per
                group.
                This is the shape that a human reader often wants to scan.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Knowing when to use each one, and how to move between them, is a
          really useful skill when you're working with data.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-shapes-long-to-wide">
        <title>Pivoting the Flamingos Data</title>

        <p>
          <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c> has been in <term>long</term> format all
          along: one row per bird, and one column per variable.
          That's why all our visualisations with <c>ggplot2</c> have worked
          without any reshaping.
          But long format isn't always what you want to <em>look</em> at, and
          reshaping between the two is a skill in its own right.
        </p>

        <p>
          Say you want a simple summary table of how many birds there are of
          each species at each lake, laid out the way someone reading a report
          would expect: lakes down the side, and species across the top.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        library(dplyr)
        library(tidyr)

        counts_long &lt;- kenya_flamingos %&gt;%
          count(lake, species)

        head(counts_long)

        #&gt;          lake          species  n
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria Greater Flamingo 10
        #&gt; 2     Bogoria  Lesser Flamingo 114
        #&gt; 3 Elementaita Greater Flamingo 30
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita  Lesser Flamingo 38
        #&gt; 5      Nakuru Greater Flamingo 26
        #&gt; 6      Nakuru  Lesser Flamingo 126
          </code>
        </program>
        <p>
          <c>counts_long</c> is exactly the shape a computer wants: one row for
          each lake and species combination, and one column holding the count.
          But it's not the easiest shape for a person to read.
          It's much easier to compare species across lakes when the species are
          in columns instead of rows.
          <c>pivot_wider()</c> turns the <c>species</c> values into column
          names.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        counts_wide &lt;- counts_long %&gt;%
          pivot_wider(names_from = species, values_from = n)

        counts_wide

        #&gt;          lake Greater Flamingo Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria               10             114
        #&gt; 2 Elementaita               30              38
        #&gt; 3      Nakuru               26             126
          </code>
        </program>
        <p>
          Now the table is really easy to read by eye, but it's no longer in a
          shape that <c>ggplot2</c> can plot directly.
        </p>

        <p>
          <term>From wide to long</term>
        </p>

        <p>
          If we reverse the process with <c>pivot_longer()</c>,
          <c>counts_wide</c> becomes plottable again.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        counts_long &lt;- counts_wide %&gt;%
          pivot_longer(cols = -lake, names_to = "species", values_to = "n")
        counts_long

        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 x 3
        #&gt;   lake        species              n
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;chr&gt;            &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1 Bogoria     Greater Flamingo    10
        #&gt; 2 Bogoria     Lesser Flamingo    114
        #&gt; 3 Elementaita Greater Flamingo    30
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita Lesser Flamingo     38
        #&gt; 5 Nakuru      Greater Flamingo    26
        #&gt; 6 Nakuru      Lesser Flamingo    126

        
ggplot(counts_long, aes(x = lake, y = n, fill = species)) + 
  geom_bar(stat = "identity") +
  labs(x = "Lake", y = "Count", fill = "Species")
          </code>
        </program>
        <figure>
          <caption>
            A bar chart with three bars comparing the number of flamingoes at
            each lake, split by species.
            Created using data in a long format.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch6_long_plot.png" width="100%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A bar chart with three bars comparing the number of flamingoes
                at each lake, split by species.
                The bar for Nakuru is the tallest, followed by Bogoria, then
                Elementaita.
                The bar for Lesser Flamingoes is taller than the bar for Greater
                Flamingoes at each lake.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          This round trip, from long to wide and back to long again, is worth
          the effort.
          Each shape serves a different reader.
          Long format serves R: <c>ggplot2</c>, <c>group_by()</c>, and joins all
          expect it.
          Wide format serves a person scanning a table by eye, or a spreadsheet
          formula that expects species to be in fixed columns.
          If you know which one a task actually needs before you write any code,
          you can save yourself exactly the kind of repeated reshaping that cost
          Gassen a day's work when JHU's columns shifted under him, and the kind
          of merging failure that hurt Sierra Leone's Ebola response.
          We look at the JHU example next.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="sec-jhu-shapes">
        <title>A Format That Grew a New Column Every Day</title>

        <p>
          In January 2020, a small team at Johns Hopkins University's Center for
          Systems Science and Engineering (CSSE) launched a public COVID-19
          dashboard, which they described that May in a peer-reviewed paper
          (Dong, Du, and Gardner, 2020).
          Within weeks, it had become one of the most-used sources of COVID-19
          data in the world: the map that news outlets, governments, and
          researchers everywhere were pulling numbers from.
          The data behind it was published openly, and it started out
          <term>wide</term>: one row for each country or region, and one column
          for every date, holding that location's total case count so far on
          that day.
        </p>

        <p>
          <alert>A table with no final column</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          Most wide tables have a fixed set of columns that you know in advance.
          This one didn't.
          Every single day the pandemic went on, the dataset needed one more
          column than the day before, for as long as anyone was tracking the
          outbreak.
          Anyone who wanted to do more than look at the day's totals (plot a
          trend, work out a weekly change, or merge in a country's population to
          calculate a rate) first had to reshape the table from wide to long.
          And they had to do it again every time a new day's column was added.
          Wide format wasn't <em>hard</em> to use.
          It was hard to use <em>again and again, at a growing scale, with no
          end in sight.</em>
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-covid-wide-format">
          <caption>
            Confirmed COVID-19 cases worldwide, 22 January to 2 February 2020.
            Each row represents a country or region, while each date is stored
            as a separate column.
            As more days are added, the dataset quickly becomes very wide.
            This is one of three datasets in the original collection: Confirmed
            Cases, Deaths, and Recovered.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/covid_wide_format.png" width="60%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A screenshot of a wide COVID-19 dataset.
                Rows represent countries and regions, with columns for location,
                latitude and longitude followed by daily confirmed case counts
                from 22 January 2020 onwards.
                The many date columns illustrate how the dataset becomes
                increasingly wide as observations are recorded for each new day.
                This is the "Confirmed Cases" dataset; equivalent datasets were
                provided for deaths and recovered cases.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          <alert>Reshaping it themselves, and having the ground move</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          For a dashboard that people glanced at once a day, wide format worked
          perfectly.
          But some people needed to do more than glance at the data, so they
          reshaped it themselves.
        </p>

        <p>
          On 23 March 2020, the researcher Joachim Gassen published R code that
          took JHU's wide file and reshaped it to long (one row per country per
          date), specifically so it could be merged with other data.
          A wide table with a different date in every column has no single
          column that two datasets can agree to join on, but a long table does.
          Gassen's code went a step further, and attached standardised
          <term>ISO3c</term> country codes to every row.
          Without a shared identifier like that, nobody could reliably bring in
          each country's population to turn raw case counts into a rate per
          person.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-covid-long-format">
          <caption>
            The same confirmed-case numbers as
            <xref ref="fig-covid-wide-format"/>, reshaped to long format: one
            row per country per date, with a standardised country code attached
            for merging.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/covid_long_format.png" width="60%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A long-format COVID-19 dataset with one row per country per
                date.
                Columns include a standardised ISO3c country code, country name,
                date, and confirmed case count, instead of one column per date.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          His reshaping lasted about a day.
          On 24 March 2020, Gassen published a follow-up post: <q>Just hours
          after my old blog post about tidying Johns Hopkins CSSE Covid-19 data
          the team has changed their time-series table data structure.</q>
          JHU had restructured the columns in their wide file, adding latitude
          and longitude, and reorganising how US states were broken down.
          It was a real improvement, but it broke every piece of code written
          for the old column layout, including Gassen's.
          He rewrote his code and republished it the same day.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-jhu-shapes-lesson">
          <title>
            Reshaping is a job that keeps coming back
          </title>

          <p>
            JHU never made its published file long, and it didn't need to,
            because wide really was the right shape for a dashboard people
            checked once a day.
            The people who needed it long (to merge, to calculate rates, to plot
            trends across countries) had to reshape it themselves.
            And because they didn't control the source format, a change nobody
            warned them about broke their reshaping within a day of them
            publishing it.
            So think of wide and long as two shapes serving two different
            readers, rather than a right and a wrong version of the same table.
            And whoever needs the other shape is the one who ends up doing the
            reshaping, again and again, for as long as the source keeps changing
            underneath them.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Dong, E., Du, H.
          and Gardner, L.
          (2020).
          <q>An interactive web-based dashboard to track COVID-19 in real
        time.</q>
          <em>The Lancet Infectious Diseases</em>, 20(5), 533 <ndash/> 534.
          Gassen, J.
          (23 March 2020).
          <q>Tidying the John Hopkins Covid-19 data.</q> Blog post.
          Gassen, J.
          (24 March 2020).
          <q>Tidying the new Johns Hopkins Covid-19
        time-series datasets.</q>
          Blog post.
          Data repository:
          <url href="https://github.com/CSSEGISandData/COVID-19" visual="github.com/CSSEGISandData/COVID-19"/>.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-append">
      <title>Appending Data</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          Appending is the simplest shape operation: you take two datasets that
          describe the same kind of thing, and stack them into one.
          It sounds straightforward, and when everything is consistent, it is!
          But the column names, data types, and variable definitions all need to
          match.
          When they don't, you either get an error, or (more dangerously) a
          silent expansion that looks fine until you try to use it.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-shapes-append">
        <title>Appending the 2016 and 2017 Kenya Road Accidents Data</title>

        <p>
          In <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/> and <xref
          ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, we worked with the Kenya Road Accidents
          database for April to June 2016.
          It turns out the same Excel file has a second sheet, with data from
          2017.
          This is a natural appending problem: two datasets recording the same
          kind of information, in the same format, for different time periods.
          We want to combine them into one.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        library(dplyr)
        library(tidyr)
        library(readxl)

        kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- read_excel("kenya-accidents-database.xlsx")
        kenya_accidents_2017 &lt;- read_excel("kenya-accidents-database.xlsx",
                                            sheet = "2017")
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Before we combine anything, it's worth looking at both datasets side
          by side to see how they compare.
          Which columns do they share?
          What's different?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        head(kenya_accidents_2016)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 13
        #&gt;   `TIME 24 HOURS` `BASE/SUB BASE` COUNTY       ROAD            PLACE
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt; 1 630             KITUI           MAKUENI      KITUI-ITHOKWE   KITUI SCHOOL
        #&gt; 2 830             VOI             TAITA TAVETA MOMBASA-NAIROBI IKANGA
        #&gt; 3 1330            MARIAKANI       KILIFI       MOMBASA-NAIROBI KATOLANI
        #&gt; 4 2100            ONGATA RONGAI   NAKURU       NAKURU-NAIROBI  MAASAI LODGE
        #&gt; 5 1900            MATUU           MACHAKOS     MATUU-MWINGI    KIVANI
        #&gt; 6 2130            NYANDO          HOMA BAY     KATITO-KENDU B… PAP ONDITI
        #&gt; # ... with 8 more variables: BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS &lt;chr&gt;, GENDER &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   AGE &lt;chr&gt;, CAUSE CODE &lt;dbl&gt;, VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, NO. &lt;dbl&gt;,
        #&gt; #   Date DD/MM/YYYY &lt;dttm&gt;, ...13 &lt;chr&gt;

        head(kenya_accidents_2017)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 15
        #&gt;   `TIME 24 HOURS` `BASE/SUB BASE` COUNTY  ROAD  PLACE `MV INVOLVED`
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt; 1 745             KISUMU          KISUMU  NAIR… KASA… KBS 163T/ZD …
        #&gt; 2 1430            NAROK           NAROK   NARO… NARO… KBS 518A HON…
        #&gt; 3 1515            LONDIANI        KERICHO KERI… JUBE… KTCB 472K JO…
        #&gt; 4 1600            KIMILILI        BUNGOMA BOKO… BITU… KBW 374M TOY…
        #&gt; 5 1747            NAKURU          NAKURU  PIPE… PIPE… KMDQ 433R DA…
        #&gt; 6 1830            ONGATA RONGAI   KAJIADO MAGA… CHAP… KBG 936M T/M…
        #&gt; # ... with 9 more variables: BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   NAME OF VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, GENDER &lt;chr&gt;, AGE &lt;chr&gt;, CAUSE CODE &lt;dbl&gt;,
        #&gt; #   VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, NO. &lt;dbl&gt;, Date DD/MM/YYYY &lt;dttm&gt;, ...15 &lt;dttm&gt;
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The two datasets share 12 columns, with the same names and the same
          data types: <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c>, <c>COUNTY</c>, <c>GENDER</c>,
          <c>AGE</c>, and so on.
          The 2016 data has a 13th column (<c>...13</c>), which we found out in
          <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/> is just a stray note.
          The 2017 data has two extra columns that 2016 doesn't have, <c>MV
          INVOLVED</c> (the vehicle registration number) and <c>NAME OF
          VICTIM</c>, plus its own stray column (<c>...15</c>).
          We drop the stray columns from both before we go any further.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016$`...13` &lt;- NULL
        kenya_accidents_2017$`...15` &lt;- NULL
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          <alert>When the columns match: a clean append</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          In <c>dplyr</c>, <c>bind_rows()</c> stacks two data frames on top of
          each other: the rows of the second go underneath the rows of the
          first.
          It's the computer version of printing out both spreadsheets and
          stapling them together.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_all &lt;- bind_rows(kenya_accidents_2016, 
                                          kenya_accidents_2017)

        head(kenya_accidents_all, 10)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 10 × 14
        #&gt;    `TIME 24 HOURS` `BASE/SUB BASE` COUNTY       ROAD            PLACE
        #&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt;  1 630             KITUI           MAKUENI      KITUI-ITHOKWE   KITUI SCHOOL
        #&gt;  2 830             VOI             TAITA TAVETA MOMBASA-NAIROBI IKANGA
        #&gt;  3 1330            MARIAKANI       KILIFI       MOMBASA-NAIROBI KATOLANI
        #&gt;  4 2100            ONGATA RONGAI   NAKURU       NAKURU-NAIROBI  MAASAI LODGE
        #&gt;  5 1900            MATUU           MACHAKOS     MATUU-MWINGI    KIVANI
        #&gt;  6 2130            NYANDO          HOMA BAY     KATITO-KENDU B… PAP ONDITI
        #&gt;  7 1200            MWEA            KIRINYAGA    MAKUTANO-MUMB… MUMAI
        #&gt;  8 1330            NAKURU          NAKURU       KENYA-UGANDA    NEAR NAKURU
        #&gt;  9 1930            KAYOLE          NAIROBI      EASTERN BYPASS  NEAR KAYOLE
        #&gt; 10 2030            THIKA           KIAMBU       NYERI ROAD      BLUE POSTS
        #&gt; # ... with 4 more variables: BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS &lt;chr&gt;, GENDER &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   AGE &lt;chr&gt;, CAUSE CODE &lt;dbl&gt;, VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, NO. &lt;dbl&gt;,
        #&gt; #   Date DD/MM/YYYY &lt;dttm&gt;, MV INVOLVED &lt;chr&gt;, NAME OF VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          It works!
          Notice that the columns that only exist in the 2017 data (<c>MV
          INVOLVED</c> and <c>NAME OF VICTIM</c>) are in the combined dataset,
          filled with <c>NA</c> for all the 2016 rows.
          <c>bind_rows()</c> handles this really nicely: it creates a column for
          every column in either dataset, and fills the gaps with missing
          values.
          No data is lost, and there are no errors.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can check the append worked by counting the rows from each year.
          If both years are there, we should see rows from 2016 and from 2017.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        library(ggplot2)
        library(lubridate)

        kenya_accidents_all %&gt;%
          mutate(year = lubridate::year(`Date DD/MM/YYYY`)) %&gt;%
          count(year)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 2 × 2
        #&gt;    year     n
        #&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1  2016   378
        #&gt; 2  2017   740
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          There are 378 rows from 2016 and 740 from 2017, so both years are
          there, as expected.
          This is a quick check that's worth doing after every append: make sure
          the row count is what you expected, and that the data from each source
          is actually there.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's also worth checking the columns.
          The 2016 data had 12 columns and the 2017 data had 14, but the
          combined dataset doesn't have 12 + 14 = 26 columns.
          It has 14, because most of the columns are shared, so they only appear
          once.
          The two extra columns from 2017 (<c>MV INVOLVED</c> and <c>NAME OF
          VICTIM</c>) are added to the shared set, giving 14 in total.
          If a column appeared in one dataset but not the other, and it really
          was a different variable (and not just a naming inconsistency), you'd
          expect to see it as an extra column, filled with <c>NA</c>s for the
          dataset that didn't have it.
          So checking the number of columns after an append, and looking closely
          at any columns you didn't expect, is a quick way to catch naming
          mismatches before they cause problems later.
        </p>

        <p>
          <alert>When the column names don't match</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          A clean append depends on both datasets using the same column names
          for the same variables.
          In the Ebola response, one problem could easily have been different
          districts using different names for the same field, and then the files
          couldn't be combined correctly.
          We can recreate that problem on purpose.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let's pretend the 2016 data had been collected with a column called
          <c>SEX</c> instead of <c>GENDER</c>.
          We can simulate that by renaming it:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- dplyr::rename(kenya_accidents_2016, 
                                               "SEX" = "GENDER")
        names(kenya_accidents_2016)
        #&gt;  [1] "TIME 24 HOURS"          "BASE/SUB BASE"         
        #&gt;  [3] "COUNTY"                 "ROAD"                  
        #&gt;  [5] "PLACE"                  "BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS"
        #&gt;  [7] "SEX"                    "AGE"                   
        #&gt;  [9] "CAUSE CODE"             "VICTIM"                
        #&gt; [11] "NO."                    "Date DD/MM/YYYY"
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          So what happens when we append now?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        bind_rows(kenya_accidents_2016, kenya_accidents_2017)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 10 × 16
        #&gt;    `TIME 24 HOURS` `BASE/SUB BASE` COUNTY  ROAD  PLACE
        #&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt;  1 630             KITUI           MAKUENI KITU… KITU…
        #&gt;  2 830             VOI             TAITA … MOMB… IKAN…
        #&gt;  3 1330            MARIAKANI       KILIFI  MOMB… KATO…
        #&gt; # ... with 7 more variables: BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   SEX &lt;chr&gt;, AGE &lt;chr&gt;, CAUSE CODE &lt;dbl&gt;, VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   NO. &lt;dbl&gt;, Date DD/MM/YYYY &lt;dttm&gt;, MV INVOLVED &lt;chr&gt;,
        #&gt; #   NAME OF VICTIM &lt;chr&gt;, GENDER &lt;chr&gt;
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          There's no error, but look carefully at the columns.
          <c>SEX</c> and <c>GENDER</c> both appear in the combined dataset, as
          separate columns.
          R has no way of knowing that <c>SEX</c> in the 2016 data and
          <c>GENDER</c> in the 2017 data are the same variable.
          As far as R is concerned, they're two different column names, so they
          become two different columns.
          The 2016 rows have values in <c>SEX</c> and <c>NA</c> in
          <c>GENDER</c>, and the 2017 rows have values in <c>GENDER</c> and
          <c>NA</c> in <c>SEX</c>.
          All the information is still there, but it's now split across two
          columns that should be one.
          Any analysis of sex or gender would need to bring them back together
          first.
        </p>

        <p>
          The best fix is to make the column names match before you append:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- dplyr::rename(kenya_accidents_2016, 
                                               "GENDER" = "SEX")
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          <alert>When the column types don't match</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          A more subtle problem comes up when both datasets use the same column
          name, but store the values as different types.
          The date column is a good example.
          In both datasets, <c>Date DD/MM/YYYY</c> is read in as a
          <c>POSIXct</c> datetime object.
          But suppose we convert it to a simpler <c>Date</c> type in the 2016
          data as part of cleaning, like we did in <xref
          ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, and leave the 2017 data as it is:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
          mutate(`Date DD/MM/YYYY` = as.Date(`Date DD/MM/YYYY`))

        class(kenya_accidents_2016$`Date DD/MM/YYYY`)
        #&gt; [1] "Date"

        class(kenya_accidents_2017$`Date DD/MM/YYYY`)
        #&gt; [1] "POSIXct" "POSIXt"
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Both of these are dates, but R stores them differently.
          So what happens when we append?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        bind_rows(kenya_accidents_2016, kenya_accidents_2017)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          It works!
          <c>bind_rows()</c> silently converts both to <c>dttm</c> (datetime)
          format to sort out the conflict.
          That's convenient, but notice the word <q>silently</q>: R made a
          decision about your data without telling you.
          Here, that decision doesn't do any harm, but in general, it's worth
          checking the types in the combined dataset after you append, instead
          of assuming everything was sorted out correctly.
        </p>

        <p>
          Not every type mismatch gets sorted out so nicely.
          Convert <c>AGE</c> to numeric in the 2016 data with
          <c>as.numeric()</c>, like we did in <xref
          ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, and leave it as character in the 2017
          data.
          We'll do this on a copy of the 2016 data, so the original stays as it
          is for later in the chapter.
          This time, you get an error when you try to append:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016_numeric &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
          mutate(AGE = as.numeric(AGE))

        class(kenya_accidents_2016_numeric$AGE)
        #&gt; [1] "numeric"

        class(kenya_accidents_2017$AGE)
        #&gt; [1] "character"

        bind_rows(kenya_accidents_2016_numeric, kenya_accidents_2017)
        #&gt; Error in `bind_rows()`:
        #&gt;   ! Can't combine `..1$AGE` &lt;double&gt; and `..2$AGE` &lt;character&gt;.
        #&gt; Run `rlang::last_trace()` to see where the error occurred.
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Unlike the dates, R can't decide by itself whether a number and a
          piece of text are the same thing, so instead of guessing, it refuses.
          And this error is actually really helpful: it tells you exactly which
          column is causing the problem, and what the conflict is.
          The fix is to make the types match before you append, either by
          converting <c>AGE</c> to numeric in both datasets, or by leaving it as
          character in both.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-append-lesson">
          <title>
            Appending reveals assumptions you didn't know you'd made.
          </title>

          <p>
            A clean append needs both datasets to agree on three things: the
            same column names for the same variables, the same data types for
            each column, and the same definition of what a row is.
            When any of those breaks, you either get an error (which at least
            tells you something's gone wrong), or a silent expansion, with extra
            columns appearing and types being quietly changed, which might not.
            The two sheets of the Kenya road accidents data pass all three,
            which is why the append works so cleanly.
            That's no accident: it's because the same data collection format was
            used in both years.
          </p>
        </insight>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-binding-by-rows">
        <title>
          Binding by Rows: Stacking Farmer Lists for Fertiliser Vouchers
        </title>

        <p>
          Every planting season, the Kenyan government subsidises fertiliser for
          smallholder farmers, and distributes it through National Cereals and
          Produce Board (NCPB) depots.
          A farmer registers once, and gets a voucher by SMS.
          They turn up at the depot with their phone and their ID, the clerk
          checks them against the system, and they walk away with fertiliser at
          a fraction of the market price.
          For a smallholder farmer, that price difference really matters.
          It can be the difference between planting a full acre and planting
          half of one.
        </p>

        <p>
          Behind that one lookup at the depot counter, there's a whole chain of
          everyday data work.
          Registration lists have to be combined into one national list, and
          that list has to be checked against each farmer's ID and phone details
          before a voucher is issued.
        </p>

        <p>
          One way to build a national registry is to take every local list of
          registered farmers, and stack them into one table, one after another.
          For that to work cleanly, every list has to agree on its columns: the
          same column names, meaning the same things, all the way down.
          If one office's spreadsheet calls a column <c>phone_number</c>, and
          another calls the same thing <c>mobile_no</c>, the software won't stop
          and ask you what to do.
          It'll create a new column for the name it hasn't seen before, and fill
          it with <term>NA</term> (<q>not available</q>, the standard way
          software marks a gap in the data) everywhere that column doesn't
          apply.
          A registry that should have five clean columns quietly grows extra
          ones that nobody asked for, and a farmer's real phone number can end
          up in one of two different columns, depending on which office
          registered them.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-schema-drift">
          <title>Computers Only Match Characters</title>

          <p>
            Two records that look identical to a person can be completely
            different to a computer, if the column names, spacing, or exact text
            differ by even one character.
            An append needs every table to agree on its columns before you stack
            them.
            Ideally, it also needs a rule for what counts as <q>the same
            real-world thing twice</q>, before it lets a duplicate in.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Kenyans.co.ke (9 February 2023).
          <q>Govt Gives Out Vouchers for Fertilizers; How to Apply.</q>
          <url href="https://www.kenyans.co.ke/news/85395-govt-gives-out-vouchers-fertilizers-how-apply" visual="kenyans.co.ke"/>.
          Ministry of Agriculture and Livestock Development (2025).
          <q>Launch of the 2025 Long Rains National Fertilizer Subsidy
          Programme.</q>
          <url href="https://kilimo.go.ke/cs-dr-andrew-karanja-has-presided-over-the-launch-of-the-2025-long-rains-national-fertilizer-subsidy-programme-nfsp-at-the-ncpb-gpc-depot-nairobi/" visual="kilimo.go.ke"/>.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="subsec-ss-shapes-definitions">
        <title>Same Column Name, Different Definition</title>

        <p>
          Now suppose we wanted to append our 2016 Kenya road crash data to some
          equivalent data from Uganda, so we could compare them.
          The two datasets might have the same column names, the same data
          types, and the same structure (a row per crash, with columns for time,
          location, victim category, and cause), so <c>bind_rows()</c> would run
          without any errors.
          But Kenya and Uganda define a road fatality differently.
          In Kenya, the NTSA seems to only record deaths at the scene, with no
          follow-up at hospitals to find out whether people later died of their
          injuries (Muguro et al., 2020).
          In Uganda, a road crash counts as fatal if someone dies at the scene,
          or within a year and a day of the crash because of their injuries
          (Nankunda and Evdorides, 2025).
          So someone who died at the scene would count as a road fatality in
          both countries.
          But someone who survived the crash and died in hospital six months
          later would count in Uganda, and not in Kenya.
          The combined data would be a mix of values collected under different
          rules, with nothing to tell you which is which.
          The append would work technically, and fail analytically.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-variable-definitions">
          <title>Same Name, Different Meaning</title>

          <p>
            When you append, software checks whether the column names and data
            types match.
            It can't check whether the data behind them was produced in
            comparable ways.
            So before you combine data from different sources, ask yourself:
            <q>are these datasets actually measuring the same reality?</q>
          </p>

          <p>
            Two columns can have exactly the same name, and still mean different
            things.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Muguro et al. (2020), cited in
          <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>, on the Kenya NTSA fatality definition.
          Nankunda, C. and Evdorides, H. (2025).
          <q>Data Availability for Road Crash Valuation in Low- and Middle-Income
          Countries: A Case Study in Uganda.</q>
          <em>Future Transportation</em>, 5(1), 29.
          <url href="https://doi.org/10.3390/futuretransp5010029" visual="doi.org/10.3390/futuretransp5010029"/>.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shapes-merge">
      <title>Merging: Bringing Different Information Together</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          Appending stacks datasets that describe the same kind of thing.
          Merging does something different: it brings together two datasets that
          describe <em>different</em> things about the same subjects.
          The Kenya road accidents data tells us where and when each crash
          happened, and who was involved.
          When we explored the data, we saw that Nairobi had the most crashes.
          But Nairobi also has the biggest population of any county in Kenya.
          So does that mean Nairobi's roads are the most dangerous, or just that
          more people use them?
          The 2019 Kenya census tells us how many people live in each county.
          Put the two datasets together, and they can answer a question that
          neither one could answer alone: <em>which counties have the most
          dangerous roads for the size of their population?</em>
          And the answer, it turns out, isn't Nairobi!
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-merge-census">
        <title>Loading the census data</title>

        <p>
          The county-level data from the 2019 Kenya Population and Housing
          Census is available directly from the Kenya National Bureau of
          Statistics (KNBS) website, and also from the Humanitarian Data
          Exchange (HDX), which is where the accidents data came from too.
          <url
          href="https://www.knbs.or.ke/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/2019-Kenya-population-and-Housing-Census-Population-households-density-by-county.xlsx">Download
          the county-level Excel file</url> and load it into R:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_census_2019 &lt;- read_excel(
          "2019-Kenya-population-and-Housing-Census-Population-households-density-by-county.xlsx"
        )

        head(kenya_census_2019)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 10
        #&gt;   `Population by Sex, Househo...` ...2  ...3  ...4  ...5  ...6  ...7  ...8  ...9  ...10
        #&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;                           &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;
        #&gt; 1 NA                              NA    NA    NA    NA    NA    NA    NA    NA    NA
        #&gt; 2 NA                              Total Sex   NA    NA    Hous… NA    NA    Land… Dens…
        #&gt; 3 County                          NA    Male  Fema… Inte… Total Conv… Grou… Sq Km Pers…
        #&gt; 4 KENYA                           4756… 2354… 2401… 1524  1214… 1204… 1008… 5808… 81.8…
        #&gt; 5 MOMBASA                         1208… 6102… 5980… 30    3784… 3762… 2127  219.… 5495…
        #&gt; 6 KWALE                           8668… 4251… 4416… 18    1731… 1728… 374   8253… 105.…
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Well, this is an interesting start!
          The spreadsheet has two rows of headers: a merged header spanning
          several columns on row 2, and the actual column names on row 3.
          The data doesn't start until row 4.
          R expects exactly one header row, so it has used the first row as
          column names, and read everything else as data.
          So we skip the first three rows, and give the columns our own names:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_census_2019 &lt;- read_excel(
          "2019-Kenya-population-and-Housing-Census-Population-households-density-by-county.xlsx",
          skip = 3
        )

        names(kenya_census_2019) &lt;- c("County", "Population", "Male", "Female",
                                       "Intersex", "Households",
                                       "Households_Conventional",
                                       "Households_Group_Quarters",
                                       "Land_Area_Sq_Km",
                                       "Density_Per_Sq_Km")
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The last row of the file is a source note, and isn't data.
          If we flick through the data, we can see that <c>Population</c> has a
          value in every row except this one.
          So we can drop that last row by removing any row where
          <c>Population</c> is <c>NA</c>.
          The file also has a row for <c>KENYA</c> as a whole.
          That's the national total, and isn't a county, so we remove it too:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        tail(kenya_census_2019)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 10
        #&gt;   County       Population    Male  Female Intersex Households ...
        #&gt; 1 HOMA BAY        1131950  539560  592367       23     262036 ...
        #&gt; 2 MIGORI          1116436  536187  580214       35     240168 ...
        #&gt; 3 KISII           1266860  605784  661038       38     308054 ...
        #&gt; 4 NYAMIRA          605576  290907  314656       13     150669 ...
        #&gt; 5 NAIROBI CITY    4397073 2192452 2204376      245    1506888 ...
        #&gt; 6 Source: 201…         NA      NA      NA       NA         NA ...

        kenya_census_2019 &lt;- kenya_census_2019 %&gt;%
          dplyr::filter(!is.na(Population), County != "KENYA")
          </code>
        </program>

        <note>
          <p>
            Notice that the census data is in wide format: one row per county,
            with separate columns for the male and female populations.
            That's the right shape for a summary table, but as we saw earlier in
            this chapter, we might want to reshape it later if we want to plot
            or analyse the data by sex.
          </p>
        </note>

        <p>
          <alert>Telling R what to join on</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          Before we join, we need to tell R which column in each dataset holds
          the shared identifier.
          In <c>dplyr</c>, the join functions have a <c>by</c> argument for
          this.
          What happens if we forget it?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_join &lt;- dplyr::full_join(kenya_accidents_2016,
                                           kenya_census_2019)
        #&gt; Error in `full_join()`:
        #&gt;   ! `by` must be supplied when `x` and `y` have no common variables.
        #&gt;   ℹ Use `cross_join()` to perform a cross-join.
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The error tells us exactly what's wrong: the two datasets don't share
          any columns with the same name, so R can't guess which columns to join
          on.
          In the accidents data, the county column is called <c>COUNTY</c>, and
          in the census, it's called <c>County</c>.
          So we tell R which columns match:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        kenya_accidents_join &lt;- dplyr::full_join(kenya_accidents_2016,
                                           kenya_census_2019,
                                           by = c("COUNTY" = "County"))
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          But when we look at the result, there's a problem.
          Here are the first five rows of the joined dataset, with the accident
          columns (from the accidents data) and the population columns (from the
          census):
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-ch6-joined-sample">
