              <cell>1,453,787</cell>
              <cell>704,089</cell>
              <cell>749,673</cell>
              <cell>115.81</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>4</cell>
              <cell>2100</cell>
              <cell>ONGATA RONGAI</cell>
              <cell>NAKURU</cell>
              <cell>NAKURU-NAIROBI</cell>
              <cell>MAASAI LODGE</cell>
              <cell>The vehicle knocked down a pedestrian</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>65</cell>
              <cell>29</cell>
              <cell>PEDESTRIAN</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>2,162,202</cell>
              <cell>1,077,272</cell>
              <cell>1,084,835</cell>
              <cell>288.11</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>5</cell>
              <cell>1900</cell>
              <cell>MATUU</cell>
              <cell>MACHAKOS</cell>
              <cell>MATUU-MWINGI</cell>
              <cell>KIVANDINI</cell>
              <cell>The vehicle overtook a M/cycle and lost control</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>A</cell>
              <cell>10</cell>
              <cell>PASSENGER</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>1,421,932</cell>
              <cell>710,707</cell>
              <cell>711,191</cell>
              <cell>235.53</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          Finally, let's check that no crash rows are missing population data:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_accidents_join %&gt;%
      filter(is.na(Population)) %&gt;%
      nrow()
    #&gt; [1] 0
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Every crash has been matched to its county's census data, so the join
          is done.
        </p>

        <p>
          <alert>What the merge makes possible</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
          With population data attached to every crash, we can now answer a
          question that the accidents data could never answer on its own: how
          many crashes were there <em>per person</em> in each county?
          Raw crash counts are always going to be higher in counties with bigger
          populations, just because more people live and travel there.
          A rate adjusts for that, and shows which counties have really
          dangerous roads, as opposed to just more traffic.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        library(ggplot2)
        library(dplyr)

        crash_rate &lt;- kenya_accidents_join %&gt;%
          group_by(COUNTY, Population) %&gt;%
          summarise(crashes = n(), .groups = "drop") %&gt;%
          mutate(crashes_per_100k = crashes / Population * 100000) %&gt;%
          arrange(desc(crashes_per_100k))

        ggplot(crash_rate,
               aes(x = reorder(COUNTY, crashes_per_100k),
                   y = crashes_per_100k)) +
          geom_col(fill = "#44575E") +
          geom_col(data = filter(crash_rate, COUNTY == "NAIROBI CITY"),
                   fill = "#F37021") +
          coord_flip() +
          labs(
            title = "Fatal road crashes per 100,000 people by county",
            subtitle = "Kenya, April–June 2016. Nairobi highlighted in orange.",
            x = NULL,
            y = "Crashes per 100,000 population"
          ) +
          theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch6-crash-rate">
          <caption>
            Fatal road crashes per 100,000 population by county, Kenya, April to
            June 2016.
            Nairobi City is highlighted in orange.
            Makueni leads by a substantial margin, followed by Embu.
            Nairobi, which had the most crashes in raw terms, ranks third.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch6_crash_rate.png" width="100%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A horizontal bar chart with county names on the y-axis and
                crashes per 100,000 population on the x-axis, ordered from
                highest to lowest rate.
                Makueni has the longest bar at around 2.6 crashes per 100,000,
                followed by Embu at around 1.6.
                Nairobi City is shown in orange and ranks third, just behind Embu,
                at around 1.6.
                Nakuru and Kiambu follow closely.
                The bars shorten progressively down to Nandi at the bottom with
                fewer than 0.2 crashes per 100,000.
                Counties with small populations but meaningful crash counts
                appear higher in the ranking than they would in a raw count
                chart.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          This chart tells a really different story from the raw crash counts.
          Nairobi had the most crashes in absolute terms (71 in this three-month
          period), so it would top any ranking based on counts alone.
          Makueni had 26 crashes, and Embu had just 10.
          In a chart of raw counts, Embu would barely show up.
          But Nairobi has a population of over 4.4 million people, Makueni has
          just under one million, and Embu has just over 600,000.
          Once we take population into account, Embu's 10 crashes work out as a
        slightly higher rate per person than Nairobi's 71 (both are around 1.6
        per 100,000, with Embu just ahead).
          And Makueni's 26 crashes, in a much smaller population than Nairobi's,
          give the highest rate of all, at around 2.6 per 100,000.
        </p>

        <p>
          And this really matters for policy.
          If a government decided where to invest in road safety based on raw
          crash counts, it would prioritise Nairobi, Nakuru, and Kiambu (the
          most populous counties, with the most crashes).
          A government working from crash rates would prioritise Makueni and
          Embu too.
          The ranking changes because the question changes, from <em>where do
          the most crashes happen?</em> to <em>where are people most at
          risk?</em>
          And the only reason we can ask that second question is because we
          merged in the population data.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-merge-lesson">
          <title>A join is only as good as its key</title>

          <p>
            This whole merge depended on the county names matching perfectly
            between the two datasets.
            A mismatch of even one character (<c>NAIROBI</c> and <c>NAIROBI
            CITY</c>, or <c>TAITA TAVETA</c> and <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c>) silently
            drops that county from the joined result.
            Without a matching key, R has no way to connect them.
            Checking the quality of your join is what tells a merge that worked
            apart from a merge that only looked like it worked.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Kenya National Bureau of Statistics (2019).
          <em>2019 Kenya Population and Housing Census, Volume I:
      Population by County and Sub-County.</em>
          Nairobi: KNBS.
          Available at:
          <url href="https://www.knbs.or.ke/2019-kenya-population-and-housing-census-results/"/>.
          Kenya Road Accidents database: openAFRICA / Humanitarian Data
          Exchange,
          <url href="https://data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database"/>.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-merging-keys">
        <title>Merging and the Idea of a Key: The Homa Bay Health System</title>

        <p>
          Homa Bay County, on the shore of Lake Victoria, has a high HIV burden,
          and a well-established HIV care programme.
          Like facilities across much of Kenya, health facilities there run on
          electronic medical record (EMR) systems.
          But a 2025 study of Homa Bay's digital health landscape found that its
          one county referral hospital was running seven of these systems, side
          by side!
          Three of the seven, all built specifically for HIV care, do share a
          common patient identifier.
          The other four cover general outpatient care, inpatient stays, the
          laboratory, and faith-based providers.
          These don't connect to any of the HIV systems, and don't reliably
          connect to each other either.
        </p>

        <p>
          To make that concrete, let's picture a patient.
          She's hypothetical, but based on the study's findings, and not on a
          specific case it reported.
          She's HIV-positive and pregnant.
          Her HIV care is recorded in the HIV system.
          Her antenatal visits are outside HIV care, so they're logged somewhere
          else.
          And if she's admitted to hospital, her inpatient stay goes somewhere
          else again.
          This isn't a stretch: the study's interviews describe exactly this
          pattern, with people <q>assigned multiple numbers for different
          purposes, i.e., at the inpatient, outpatient and antenatal
          clinics.</q>
        </p>

        <p>
          Every operation that merges tables relies on the same quiet
          assumption: that somewhere in your data, there's a column that
          reliably means <q>this same real-world thing</q>, everywhere it
          appears.
          That column is called a <term>key</term>.
          Homa Bay's hospital records show what happens when that's only true
          for part of a system.
          TODO: we saw exactly how this can go wrong in the illustrative
          example, when county names failed to match
        </p>

        <p>
          The 2025 study found that in most of the county's EMR systems, patient
          identifiers were created by hand, and in 97% of facilities, they were
          allocated by hand at the patient's first visit.
          There was no county-wide identifier linking a person across every
          system they might come into contact with.
          Within HIV care, a shared identifier does exist, and it does its job.
          But step outside HIV care, and there's no column anywhere in the
          hospital that reliably means <q>this same person</q> across the rest
          of the systems she might use.
        </p>

        <p>
          The problem is that there's nothing to <term>join</term> or
          <term>merge</term> most of those correct records on.
          If you stack or link the non-HIV systems anyway, without a shared key,
          one of two things happens.
          Either the same person gets silently counted more than once across
          different systems, inflating whatever gets reported to the county or
          the ministry at the end of the month.
          Or a clinician treating her for a complication in her pregnancy has no
          way of seeing, from where they're sitting, that she's on HIV treatment
          at all, even though that exact information exists, correctly recorded,
          in the system next door.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-homa-bay-no-key">
          <title>You Cannot Join What Was Never Given a Shared Key</title>

          <p>
            A join, a stack, a reshape: every one of these operations assumes
            there's already a key linking your tables together.
            When separate systems are built independently, without agreeing in
            advance how a person, place, or thing will be identified across all
            of them, no amount of clever code can recover that link later.
            The key has to be designed in before the data is collected.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Chepkirui, M., Dellicour, S., Musuva, R., et al.
          (2025).
          Missed opportunities for digital health data use in healthcare
          decision-making: A cross-sectional digital health landscape assessment
          in Homa Bay county, Kenya.
          <foreign>PLOS Digital Health</foreign>, 4(6), e0000870.
          https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pdig.0000870
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-key-errors">
        <title>What If the Key Is Wrong? The IEBC Voter Register</title>

        <p>
          Before national elections in Kenya, the Independent Electoral and
          Boundaries Commission (IEBC) has to check its master voter register
          against other government records.
          It checks against identity records, to catch invalid or duplicate
          registrations, and against death records, to remove voters who have
          died.
          Checking one table against another like this is a <term>lookup</term>,
          or <term>join</term>, and the sensible key to join on is each person's
          National ID number.
          The register also needs its own duplicates removing every so often, a
          step usually called <term>deduplication</term>: finding rows that are
          the same person entered twice, and collapsing them into one.
        </p>

        <p>
          Both of these jobs depend completely on one thing: a key that's
          correct for every row, and unique to one person.
          Get that wrong, and a register can break in two opposite directions at
          the same time.
        </p>

        <p>
          <term>A key that isn't actually unique inflates the numbers.</term>
          If a system ever falls back on matching people using something other
          than a clean, unique identifier (say, full name and date of birth
          together), things can go wrong in a way that's easy to miss.
          Imagine ten voters share a name and date of birth, and a second table
          has three other people with that same name and date of birth.
          A join won't pick one sensible match.
          It matches every one of the ten against every one of the three, and
          gives you thirty rows, when there should have been one real match at
          most.
          Now run that same overly broad matching rule through a deduplication
          step, and it goes wrong the other way.
          Anyone who happens to share those details with someone else gets
          treated as their duplicate, and is quietly deleted, even though
          they're two completely different, completely real people.
        </p>

        <p>
          We actually saw a harmless version of this in our own join.
          The <c>NAIROBI CITY</c> row appears exactly once in the 2019 census
          data.
          But it appears 71 times in the accidents data, once for each crash
          recorded in Nairobi.
          The left join correctly matched all 71 of those crash rows to the same
          census row, repeating Nairobi's population 71 times in the joined
          dataset.
          That's exactly what we wanted!
          But one record matching many would be a serious problem in a voter
          register.
          Whether repeated matches are right or disastrous depends entirely on
          what the key represents, and what the join is meant to do.
        </p>

        <p>
          <term>A key that's untidy hides real matches.</term>
          Even with a clean, unique identifier as the key, small formatting
          differences can break things.
          One system stores an ID as a plain number.
          Another stores the same ID as text, with a leading zero kept on the
          front.
          A third has a stray space accidentally typed at the end.
          To a person, these are obviously the same ID.
          To a join or a deduplication step, they're three unrelated pieces of
          text, and no match is ever found.
        </p>

        <p>
          So what did KPMG's 2022 audit of the IEBC register actually find?
          Their preliminary report found <term>481,711</term> duplicate records
          on the register.
          It also found 246,465 deceased voters still on the register, and
          226,143 voters registered with ID numbers that didn't validly belong
          to them.
          Every one of these is a key problem.
          Duplicate records are what you get when the same person ends up on the
          register twice, and the deduplication step doesn't catch it.
          Deceased voters can only be removed if the join against death records
          actually finds a match.
          And an ID number that belongs to someone else is a key that's simply
          wrong, so any join on it links the wrong people together.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-cartesian-explosion">
          <title>A Key Is Either Too Broad or Too Untidy, Rarely Neither</title>

          <p>
            A join should never make your row count bigger than you expected.
            If it does, the key you joined on isn't unique on at least one side.
            A deduplication step should only ever remove rows you can point to
            as real duplicates.
            If entries you know are different disappear, your key was too broad.
            And if a join or deduplication misses matches that are obviously the
            same to a person, check the spacing, the leading zeros, and whether
            both sides store the value as the same type, before you trust the
            result.
          </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> KPMG (2022).
          <foreign>Preliminary audit report on the Register of Voters</foreign>,
          prepared for the Independent Electoral and Boundaries Commission
          (IEBC), as reported in the IEBC press release of 8 June 2022
          (<url href="https://www.iebc.or.ke/uploads/resources/cWyBL8fhHH.pdf" visual="iebc.or.ke"/>)
          and in <foreign>Citizen Digital</foreign>,
          <q>IEBC announces delay in publishing final voters' register as 1M names
          flagged</q>
          (<url href="https://www.citizen.digital/article/iebc-announces-delay-in-publishing-final-voters-register-as-1m-names-flagged-n299750" visual="citizen.digital"/>).
        </p>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-data-shapes">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-jhu-daily">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="insight-jhu-shapes-lesson"/> says that a new column had to
          be added to the wide dataset every single day.
          Describe, in words, what would go wrong for someone's code if it
          referred to date columns by a fixed position (for example, <q>column
          47</q>) instead of by name.
          Would switching to long format, on its own, have fixed that particular
          problem?
          Why, or why not?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-reshape-census-data">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The census data we loaded in <xref ref="ss-merge-census"/> has
          separate columns for the <c>Male</c> and <c>Female</c> populations.
          Use <c>pivot_longer()</c> to reshape it so that each county appears
          twice, once for the male population and once for the female
          population, with a new column called <c>sex</c>, and a column called
          <c>population</c> holding the count.
          Then use <c>ggplot2</c> to plot the male and female populations side
          by side for each county, with a different colour for each sex.
          Which counties have the most unequal sex distributions?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-ebola-standard">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-intro-data-shapes"/> argues that agreeing on a shape in
          advance is cheaper than trying to reconcile incompatible shapes
          afterwards.
          Suggest one practical rule that Sierra Leone's district response
          centres could have agreed on in advance (about column names, row
          structure, or file format) that would have prevented the merging problem described in <xref ref="ss-ebola-districts"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-flamingo-wide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using <xref ref="ss-shapes-long-to-wide"/> as a model, build a wide
          table of the mean <c>body_mass_g</c>, with <c>lake</c> as rows and
          <c>species</c> as columns, instead of counts.
          Which version, this one or the <c>counts_wide</c> table of counts, would you
          send to someone who wanted to read it in an email?
          And which would you give to someone who wanted to plot it in
          <c>ggplot2</c>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-shapes-gapminder-wide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <c>gapminder</c> data from <xref
          ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> is in long format: one
          row per country per year.
          Use <c>pivot_wider()</c> to build a table with one row per country,
          and one column per year, holding <c>lifeExp</c>.
          Now try plotting directly from this wide version, using the same
          <c>ggplot2</c> code from <xref ref="ss-gapminder-2d"/>.
          What breaks, and why?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>